    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d176">

                <pb facs="0322" n="210" xml:id="pg210"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 135/6–7.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Rockhill to
                        the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Peking</hi></placeName>,<date calendar="gregorian" when="1907-09-04"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">September 4, 1907</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 714.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the department’s instruction
                    No. 299, of May 31 last (file No. 135–1) and my reply thereto, No. 669, of July
                    18, 1907, on the subject of the Chinese characters to be used by the local
                    authorities in stamping deeds for property purchased for missionary purposes in
                    China, I have the honor to inclose copy of a note received from the foreign
                    office, by which it is agreed to stamp the deeds in the manner suggested by this
                    legation.</p>
                <p>A copy of this note has been sent to our consuls in China for their information
                    and guidance.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. W. Rockhill.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d176-a1.xml" xpointer="d176-a1"/>
            </div>
